      "shift": "24 Jun 2022, Shift 1",
      "status": "draft",
      "stem": "The sum of the infinite series $1 + \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{6}{3^2} + \\frac{10}{3^3} + \\frac{14}{3^4} + \\dots$ is:",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "content": "2",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "content": "3",
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "content": "4",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "content": "6",
          "is_correct": false
        }
      ],
      "solution": "Let $S = 1 + \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{6}{3^2} + \\frac{10}{3^3} + \\dots$\nThen $S - 1 = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{6}{3^2} + \\frac{10}{3^3} + \\dots$\nLet $S' = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{6}{3^2} + \\frac{10}{3^3} + \\dots$\n$\\frac{1}{3}S' = \\frac{2}{3^2} + \\frac{6}{3^3} + \\dots$\nSubtracting: $\\frac{2}{3}S' = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{4}{3^2} + \\frac{4}{3^3} + \\dots = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{4/9}{1 - 1/3} = \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nSo $S' = 2$, which gives $S = 1 + S' = 3$."
    },
    {
      "ref": "MATH-SEQ-002",
      "subtopic": "arithmetico-geometric-progression",
      "type": "numerical",
      "difficulty": 5,
      "expected_time_sec": 150,
      "source_type": "pyq",
      "exam": "jee_main",
      "year": 2022,
      "shift": "24 Jun 2022, Shift 1",
      "status": "draft",
      "stem": "Let $S_n = 1 \\cdot (n-1) + 2 \\cdot (n-2) + 3 \\cdot (n-3) + \\dots + (n-1) \\cdot 1$. If $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{6 S_n}{n^3} = L$, then the value of $L$ is :",
      "image": null,
      "answer": {
        "min": 1.0,
        "max": 1.0
      },
      "solution": "The general term is $T_r = r(n-r) = nr - r^2$ for $r=1$ to $n-1$.\n$S_n = \\sum_{r=1}^{n-1} (nr - r^2) = n \\sum_{r=1}^{n-1} r - \\sum_{r=1}^{n-1} r^2 = n \\frac{(n-1)n}{2} - \\frac{(n-1)n(2n-1)}{6}$.\n$S_n = n^3 \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{2}{6} \\right) + O(n^2) = n^3 \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\right) + O(n^2)$.\nThus, $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{6 S_n}{n^3} = 6 \\times \\frac{1}{6} = 1$."
    },
    {
